 ### Sparse Voxel Octree with Bricks
-Uses brick-based compression for memory efficiency. SVRaster uses a flat 1D array of leaves, trading compression for simpler rasterization logic.
+A hierarchical structure that compresses adjacent empty or uniform voxels into bricks to save memory. SVRaster differs by storing only leaves in a flat 1D array, trading compression for simpler, faster rasterization logic [S1]. Choose SVRaster when rendering speed and direct rasterization compatibility are prioritized over memory footprint.
 
 ### Naive Octree Node Representation
-Stores full octree nodes. SVRaster stores only leaves, reducing overhead.
+Stores full octree nodes including internal pointers and metadata. SVRaster differs by discarding internal nodes and storing only leaf voxels, reducing traversal overhead. Choose SVRaster when scene complexity is high and memory bandwidth for node traversal becomes a bottleneck.
 
 ### Dynamic Update Optimization
-Focuses on updating the structure during training. SVRaster's adaptive allocation is a specific instance of this, focusing on pruning and subdivision based on loss.
+Techniques for efficiently modifying the spatial structure during training or runtime. SVRaster's adaptive allocation is a specific instance, focusing on pruning low-contribution voxels and subdividing high-loss regions based on training view sampling density [S2]. Choose SVRaster when explicit, trainable voxel structures are required without neural field overhead.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Rendering is rasterization-based, allowing for real-time frame rates. The paper reports 121 FPS on a 3090 Ti for Mip-NeRF360 scenes, comparable to 3DGS (131 FPS) [S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Uses more GPU memory and produces larger models than 3DGS [S2]. The maximum representational resolution is $65536^3$, but actual memory usage depends on the number of active leaf voxels [S2].
-*   **Performance:** On Mip-NeRF360, SVRaster achieves LPIPS 0.219 vs 0.257 for 3DGS, and PSNR 27.33 vs 27.45 dB [S2]. On Tanks&Temples, it shows brightness boundaries and floaters due to exposure variation, with worse PSNR and FPS than 3DGS, though LPIPS is better [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Avoids popping artifacts; compatible with grid-based 3D processing (Volume Fusion, Marching Cubes); neural-free; high fidelity.
-*   **Limitations:** Sensitive to exposure variation in training data; higher memory usage than 3DGS; requires custom rasterizer.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Rendering is rasterization-based, enabling real-time frame rates. The method achieves 121 FPS on a 3090 Ti for Mip-NeRF360 scenes, comparable to 3DGS (131 FPS) [S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Uses more GPU memory and produces larger models than 3DGS [S2]. The maximum representational resolution is $65536^3$, but actual memory usage scales with the number of active leaf voxels [S2].
+*   **Performance:** On Mip-NeRF360, SVRaster achieves LPIPS 0.219 versus 0.257 for 3DGS, and PSNR 27.33 versus 27.45 dB [S2]. On Tanks&Temples, it exhibits brightness boundaries and floaters due to exposure variation, yielding worse PSNR and FPS than 3DGS, though LPIPS remains better [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Eliminates popping artifacts via disjoint voxel partitioning and direction-dependent sorting; compatible with grid-based 3D processing (Volume Fusion, Marching Cubes); neural-free representation; high fidelity [S1, S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Sensitive to exposure variation in training data; higher memory usage and model size than 3DGS; requires a custom rasterizer and does not use COLMAP sparse points [S2].
 
 ## Background
-SVRaster was proposed by NVIDIA Research (NVlabs) and presented at CVPR 2025 [S1]. It builds on the concept of sparse voxel octrees and Plenoxels, aiming to combine the efficiency of rasterization with the explicit nature of voxel representations, avoiding the limitations of Gaussian splatting (popping) and neural fields (training complexity) [S1]. The code is available at [github.com/NVlabs/svraster](https://github.com/NVlabs/svraster) [S1].
